---
interface Props {
  eyebrow?: string;
  title: string;
  lead?: string;
  primaryCta?: { label: string; href: string };
  secondaryCta?: { label: string; href: string };
}

const { eyebrow, title, lead, primaryCta, secondaryCta } = Astro.props;
---

{/*
  Representative M4 Tailwind migration (2026-08-16, corrected 2026-08-16
  post-review) — see docs/architecture/web-platform-architecture.md §5.1
  and the terra-nexus-design-system skill. Replaces this component's
  scoped <style> block with Tailwind utilities backed by the tn- token
  bridge in src/styles/tailwind.css, proving the pattern before wider (M7)
  adoption. Visual output is intentionally unchanged from the plain-CSS
  version: same gradient background, same glow, same spacing/typography —
  this is a styling-mechanism swap, not a redesign. `.container`/`.eyebrow`/
  `.btn` classes from design-system.css are kept as-is (not yet migrated;
  M7 scope) since PageHero only owns the hero-specific wrapper/spacing.

  Cascade-layer gotcha (found in post-implementation review, confirmed via
  computed styles, fixed here): Tailwind v4 emits all utilities inside
  `@layer utilities`. Per the CSS cascade-layers spec, ANY unlayered
  declaration beats ANY layered one for the same property on the same
  element, regardless of selector specificity or source order. Two
  design-system.css rules are unlayered and target properties this
  component also sets via utility classes — `.eyebrow { margin-bottom:
  var(--space-3) }` and the global `h1,h2,h3,h4,h5,h6 { margin: 0 }` reset
  — so plain `mb-4`/`mb-5` utility classes here were silently losing
  (confirmed: computed margin-bottom was 12px and 0px respectively, not
  the intended 16px/20px). Fixing this with `!important` (or Tailwind's
  `!` important-modifier) would work but isn't the right general pattern.
  Moving all of design-system.css into cascade layers would fix it site-
  wide but is a large, unproven change out of M4's scope. The correct
  local fix — same mechanism the pre-M4 scoped <style> block already used
  to win against the same global rules — is a small scoped <style> block
  below: Astro scopes it to a component-specific attribute selector, which
  is unlayered and outranks the plain global selectors on specificity.
  Only the two properties that actually conflict are overridden here;
  every other utility class above is unaffected by this issue (no other
  competing unlayered rule exists for gradients/padding/gap/etc. on a bare
  `<section>`/`<div>`). Revisit whether this gotcha needs a more systemic
  answer (e.g. wrapping design-system.css's *component-pattern* rules,
  not its base reset, in a named layer) when M7 does its wider migration.

  Mobile padding, checked (found NOT a regression): design-system.css has a
  `@media (max-width: 48rem) { .page-hero { padding-block: var(--space-12)
  var(--space-10) } }` rule that looks, from source alone, like it should
  reduce padding on mobile. It never actually did for this component,
  before or after M4 — confirmed via computed styles, not inferred: the
  pre-M4 scoped <style> block's own `.page-hero { padding-block:
  var(--space-20) var(--space-16) }` rule is unlayered with equal-or-higher
  specificity (Astro's scoping attribute), so it won the media query too,
  at every viewport including 390/768. Computed padding-top/bottom at
  1440/768/390 was 80px/64px on both the pre-M4 `main` branch and this
  branch — unconditional `pt-20 pb-16` below is correct, not a bug.
*/}
<section class="relative overflow-hidden border-b border-tn-border bg-[image:var(--gradient-surface)] pt-20 pb-16">
  <div
    class="pointer-events-none absolute -top-[30%] -right-[15%] h-[50rem] w-[50rem] bg-[radial-gradient(circle,rgba(168,208,219,0.4)_0%,transparent_65%)]"
    aria-hidden="true"
  >
  </div>
  <div class="container relative">
    {eyebrow && <span class="eyebrow page-hero-eyebrow">{eyebrow}</span>}
    <h1 class="page-hero-title max-w-[var(--max-width-text)]">{title}</h1>
    {lead && (
      // leading-[var(--line-relaxed)], not Tailwind's built-in leading-relaxed
      // (1.625) — design-system.css's --line-relaxed is 1.7, a different
      // value; using the token keeps this pixel-identical to the pre-M4 CSS.
      <p class="max-w-[var(--max-width-text)] text-lg text-tn-text-secondary leading-[var(--line-relaxed)]">{lead}</p>
    )}
    {(primaryCta || secondaryCta) && (
      <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-4">
        {primaryCta && <a href={primaryCta.href} class="btn btn-primary">{primaryCta.label}</a>}
        {secondaryCta && <a href={secondaryCta.href} class="btn btn-secondary">{secondaryCta.label}</a>}
      </div>
    )}
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* Unlayered on purpose — see the cascade-layer comment above. These are
     the only two overrides this component needs; everything else is a
     Tailwind utility. Values are the same tokens design-system.css itself
     defines, not new/hardcoded ones. */
  .page-hero-eyebrow {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }
  .page-hero-title {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-5);
  }
</style>
